  <data name="Crash_JavaTooOld_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Java is too old</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_JavaTooOld_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Part of the game needs Java {0} or newer. In the instance's Settings tab, clear the Java path so Fluorite picks a matching Java, or choose Java {0} or newer.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_JavaTooNew_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Java is too new for this version</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_JavaTooNew_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>This Minecraft version or mod loader doesn't run on new Java versions; old Forge and LaunchWrapper versions need Java 8. Clear the Java path in the instance's Settings tab so Fluorite picks a matching Java, or choose Java 8.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ForgeNeedsOlderJava8_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>This Forge build doesn't work with this Java</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ForgeNeedsOlderJava8_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>This Forge build has a bug with newer Java 8 updates. Update Forge to the newest build for this Minecraft version on the Version tab.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OpenJ9_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>OpenJ9 Java isn't supported</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OpenJ9_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Minecraft mods don't work with the OpenJ9 Java VM. Clear the Java path in the instance's Settings tab, or choose a HotSpot Java such as Microsoft, Adoptium or Zulu.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_Java32Bit_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>32-bit Java can't use enough memory</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_Java32Bit_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>32-bit Java is limited to about 1 GB. Install 64-bit Java, or clear the Java path in the instance's Settings tab so Fluorite installs one.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_HeapReserveFailed_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Java couldn't get the memory it was given</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_HeapReserveFailed_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows couldn't set aside the maximum memory for Java. Lower the maximum memory (or turn on automatic memory in Settings), close other programs, or make the page file bigger.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_SystemOutOfMemory_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows ran out of memory</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_SystemOutOfMemory_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The PC ran out of memory while the game was running. Lower the maximum memory so Windows keeps enough, close other programs, or make the page file bigger.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OutOfMemory_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The game ran out of memory</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OutOfMemory_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Minecraft used all the memory it was given. Raise the maximum memory in the instance's Settings tab (or turn on automatic memory in Settings), or remove some mods, shader packs or high-resolution resource packs.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_GcConflict_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Two garbage collectors are selected</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_GcConflict_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The JVM arguments pick more than one garbage collector. Remove the extra -XX:+Use…GC argument from the JVM arguments.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_JvmOptionInvalid_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Java didn't accept a JVM argument</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_JvmOptionInvalid_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>This Java doesn't recognise “{0}”. Remove it from the JVM arguments (in Settings, or the instance's Settings tab).</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_FabricIncompatibleMods_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The loader can't load these mods together</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_FabricIncompatibleMods_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Some mods are missing a dependency, need other versions or conflict with each other. What the loader suggests:</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_MissingDependencies_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Mods are missing dependencies</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_MissingDependencies_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Some mods need other mods, or other versions of them, that aren't installed. What the loader reported:</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_DuplicateMods_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A mod is installed twice</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_DuplicateMods_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The same mod is in the mods folder more than once (often two versions of it). Keep one copy of each; the Mods tab marks duplicates.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_LoaderTooOld_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A mod needs a newer mod loader</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_LoaderTooOld_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>A mod was made for a newer Forge or NeoForge (it asks for {0}). Update the loader on the Version tab, or install the version of the mod made for this one.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ModCrashedLoading_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A mod crashed while loading</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ModCrashedLoading_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0} failed while the game was starting. Update it, make sure it's made for this Minecraft version and mod loader, or remove it to check.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_MixinFailed_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A mod couldn't patch the game</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_MixinFailed_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0} failed to apply its changes to Minecraft. It's usually made for another Minecraft or loader version, or clashes with another mod. Update or remove it.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OptiFineNeoForge_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>OptiFine doesn't support NeoForge</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OptiFineNeoForge_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Remove OptiFine. For the same features on NeoForge, use Embeddium or Sodium with Oculus or Iris.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OptiFineIncompatible_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>OptiFine doesn't fit this setup</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_OptiFineIncompatible_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>This OptiFine build doesn't match the Forge version or another mod. Use the OptiFine build made for this Forge version (see OptiFine's changelog), or replace OptiFine with Embeddium or Sodium and Oculus or Iris.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_RtssSodium_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>RivaTuner doesn't work with Sodium</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_RtssSodium_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>RivaTuner Statistics Server (often installed with MSI Afterburner) is running, and Sodium can't run alongside it. Close it, or add an exception for javaw.exe in it.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_GraphicsDriver_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The graphics driver can't run Minecraft</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_GraphicsDriver_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The graphics driver doesn't support the OpenGL features Minecraft needs, or it's out of date. Install the latest driver from your GPU maker (Intel, AMD or NVIDIA) rather than from Windows Update. On a PC with two GPUs, set Graphics card to High performance in Settings.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_GpuDriverCrash_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The graphics driver crashed</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_GpuDriverCrash_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The game crashed inside the graphics driver ({0}). Update the driver from your GPU maker. If it keeps happening, remove shader packs or rendering mods (Sodium, Iris, OptiFine) one at a time to find the trigger.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_NativeLibrary_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A native library couldn't load</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_NativeLibrary_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>A library with native code (such as LWJGL) didn't load, often because Java's architecture (x64 or ARM64) doesn't match it or antivirus removed it. Delete the natives folder in the instance's folder and launch again; if that doesn't help, clear the Java path so Fluorite picks a matching Java.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_CorruptFile_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A file is damaged</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_CorruptFile_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>A game, library or mod file is incomplete or corrupt. If it's a mod, download it again; otherwise delete the file named below and launch again so it's downloaded again.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_SignatureMismatch_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A signed file was changed</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_SignatureMismatch_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>A file with a signature was modified, usually by mods copied into the game jar. Undo those changes, or delete the version's folder so it's downloaded again.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ConfigCorrupt_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A mod's config file is damaged</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ConfigCorrupt_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>A config file couldn't be read, usually because an earlier crash left it empty. Delete the file named below from the config folder; the mod creates a new one.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ExtractedMods_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Mods were unzipped</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ExtractedMods_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The mods folder has unzipped mod folders. Put the .jar files themselves in the mods folder, without extracting them.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_IncompleteLoader_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The mod loader install is incomplete</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_IncompleteLoader_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Files from the Forge or NeoForge installation are missing. Reinstall the loader: choose a loader version again on the Version tab and apply it.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_MainClassMissing_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The game's starting point is missing</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_MainClassMissing_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Java couldn't find {0}, so the version's files are incomplete. Reinstall the mod loader on the Version tab, or apply the Minecraft version again.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_InvalidModuleName_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A mod file has an unusable name</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_InvalidModuleName_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Forge can't load a mod whose file name has no Latin letters or digits. Rename that .jar file in the mods folder (for example to the mod's English name).</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_IdLimit_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Too many blocks or items</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_IdLimit_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>This Minecraft version holds a limited number of blocks and items, and the mods add more. Remove some mods, or add a mod that lifts the limit (such as JustEnoughIDs or RoughlyEnoughIDs).</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_TickingEntity_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>An entity crashes the world</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_TickingEntity_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The entity {0} at {1} crashes the game whenever the world loads. Restore a backup of the world (Worlds tab), or remove the entity with a world editor such as MCA Selector.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_TickingBlockEntity_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>A block crashes the world</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_TickingBlockEntity_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The block {0} at {1} crashes the game whenever the world loads. Restore a backup of the world (Worlds tab), or remove the block with a world editor such as MCA Selector.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ManualCrash_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Crashed on purpose</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_ManualCrash_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>This crash was triggered with the debug keys (F3 + C held for 10 seconds). Nothing is wrong.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_SuspectedMods_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Minecraft suspects these mods</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_SuspectedMods_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>The crash report names {0}. Try updating or removing them.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_NativeCrash_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Java crashed in native code</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_NativeCrash_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Java itself crashed, usually in a graphics driver, an overlay (Discord, RivaTuner, MSI Afterburner, recording software) or a mod with native code. Update the graphics driver and turn off overlays; Java's crash log has the details.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_CodeMissing_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Code the game looked for isn't there</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_CodeMissing_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Something called code that doesn't exist ({0}). Usually a mod is made for another version of Minecraft, of the mod loader or of a library mod it needs. Check the mods you added or updated last.</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_NonAsciiPath_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>The game folder's path has non-English characters</value></data>
  <data name="Crash_NonAsciiPath_Advice" xml:space="preserve"><value>Some older versions and mods fail when the path has characters outside English ({0}). If nothing else explains the crash, move the instance to a folder with a plain path.</value></data>
